preConditionHook:
// The market has attempted and failed to break the same side of the range at
// least minFailedAttempts times inside attemptLookback bars. Four sources
// read that as 'now expect the OPPOSITE direction', one as 'now permit the
// SAME-side breakout, this attempt is the one that goes'. Those are one
// counter under the per-placement NEGATE and side toggles, not two rules.
//
// 'Failed breakout attempt' is undefined in the sources, so the bar-level
// definition is taken from FailedBreakoutReversal and INLINED verbatim: for
// each candidate bar k in 1..failBars the level is the extreme of the
// rangeBars bars ending one bar before it -- Lowest(l, rangeBars)[k + 1],
// open-coded because that is a function-history reference at a VARIABLE
// offset, and Extremes() is EL source and is exactly this loop, so the
// comparisons carry the EL relational tolerance -- and the event is a close
// beyond that level followed by this bar's close back inside. The long side
// counts failed DOWNSIDE breakouts, as that rule's long side does.
//
// ONE FAILURE IS ONE COUNT, not one per bar: brokeDown stays true for several
// consecutive bars while the same failure sits inside the failBars window, so
// the counted event is the FRESH one -- true now, false last bar. That is the
// MomentumChange crossing idiom, which the corpus keeps whole for exactly
// this reason. prevBrokeDown / prevBrokeUp mirror the EL variables' [1]
// history and are therefore updated at the END of the hook, after the fresh
// test has read them; they start false exactly as the EL variables do.
//
// CountIF over a VARIABLE, not over a price expression: the counted argument
// is the plain variable freshDown, whose own history is what CountIF reads,
// so there is no shifting-offset subtlety and no function-history reference.
// The window is bars 0..attemptLookback-1, current bar INCLUDED (CountIF's
// measured convention), and warm-up counts over the variable's ZERO-FILLED
// history. The mirror is BarRangeAboveStd's ring buffer, which starts zeroed
// and re-seeds when the length changes, for the same reason: recomputing the
// count from price would use bars EasyLanguage never saw.
//
// Kept apart from SecondFailedSwingBreakoutReversal, whose level is a
// CONFIRMED SWING PIVOT and whose count is fixed at two by an age counter;
// this one counts to any threshold against a Donchian level. Deepest bar
// touched is rangeBars + failBars of price, plus attemptLookback - 1 bars of
// variable history.
brokeDown = false;
brokeUp = false;
for (int k = 1; k <= (int)failBars; ++k) {
    double lvlLow = low[k + 1];
    double lvlHigh = high[k + 1];
    for (int j = k + 2; j <= k + (int)rangeBars; ++j) {
        if (el_lt(low[j], lvlLow)) lvlLow = low[j];
        if (el_gt(high[j], lvlHigh)) lvlHigh = high[j];
    }
    if (el_lt(close[k], lvlLow) && el_gt(close[0], lvlLow)) brokeDown = true;
    if (el_gt(close[k], lvlHigh) && el_lt(close[0], lvlHigh)) brokeUp = true;
}
freshDown = brokeDown && !prevBrokeDown;
freshUp = brokeUp && !prevBrokeUp;
if (downHistory.size() != (std::size_t)(int)attemptLookback) {
    downHistory.assign((std::size_t)(int)attemptLookback, 0.0);
    upHistory.assign((std::size_t)(int)attemptLookback, 0.0);
    histCursor = 0;
}
downHistory[histCursor] = freshDown ? 1.0 : 0.0;
upHistory[histCursor] = freshUp ? 1.0 : 0.0;
histCursor = (histCursor + 1) % downHistory.size();
cntDown = 0.0;
for (double v : downHistory) {
    cntDown += v;
}
cntUp = 0.0;
for (double v : upHistory) {
    cntUp += v;
}
prevBrokeDown = brokeDown;
prevBrokeUp = brokeUp;
